  "id": "Compound::DB00997 - Disease::DOID:10534",
  "verbalization": "Doxorubicin is implicated in the development of Stomach Cancer through a chain of interactions involving Methotrexate, which acts as a common mediator in multiple pathways. The evidence suggests that Doxorubicin causes Pneumocystis Jirovecii Pneumonia, which in turn is caused by Methotrexate, and Methotrexate itself is used to treat Stomach Cancer. This indicates a potential mechanism where Doxorubicin may indirectly contribute to Stomach Cancer by promoting the use of Methotrexate, which has a known therapeutic effect on the disease. The shared lowest common ancestors—Hydroxides and Enzyme Inhibitors—suggest a common biological pathway or functional role that links Doxorubicin and Methotrexate to their respective effects. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible dependency on Methotrexate as a key mediator in the relationship between Doxorubicin and Stomach Cancer, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
